==Bislama==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|bi|en|red}}. Cognate with {{cog|pis|red}} and {{cog|tpi|ret}}.

===Adjective===
{{head|bi|adjective|attributive|redfala|nominalised|redwan}}

# [[red#English|red]], [[purple]], [[pink]] {{gloss|having a colour in the red-purple spectrum}}
#: {{hypo|bi|perpol}}
#: {{ux|bi|Mi wantem pem wan '''red''' trak.|I want to buy a '''red''' car/truck.}}
# {{lb|bi|of hair}} [[bleached]] {{gloss|lightened in colour}}
#: {{ux|bi|Hea blong hem i '''red'''.|Her hair is '''bleached'''.}}
# {{lb|bi|of eyes}} [[bloodshot]] {{gloss|showing reddened and inflamed veins}}
#: {{hypo|bi|redae}}
#: {{ux|bi|Ae blong mi i '''red''' from mi no slip gud.|My eyes are '''bloodshot''' because I didn't sleep well.}}
# {{lb|bi|of a torch}} going [[flat]], [[dying]] {{gloss|losing battery power}}
#: {{par|bi|ded|slak}}
#: {{ux|bi|Toj ia i '''red''' finis, yu mas jajem.|This torch is going '''flat''', you need to charge it.}}
# {{lb|bi|of fruit}} [[ripe]] {{gloss|ready for reaping or gathering}}
#: {{ux|bi|Ol mango ia oli '''red''' finis, yumi save pikim olgeta.|These mangoes are '''ripe''' now, we can pick them.}}

====Usage notes====
The use of {{m|bi||red}} to cover red, purple and pink reflects traditional Melanesian colour categorisation systems, in which many indigenous languages group these colours together as variations of “red” or warm/reddish hues. Although {{m|bi|perpol}} (from {{m+|en|purple}}) can sometimes serve as a more precise term for “purple”, {{m|bi||red}} is still the most widely used term for this colour spectrum, with the specific hue usually inferred from context.

