===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[recover]], especially from an [[illness]]; to get better from an illness or from exhaustion (or sometimes from a financial loss, etc).
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.classicalpoets.org/today-a-tetraquartet-and-other-poetry-by-paul-millan/|work=The Society of Classical Poets|passage=As you '''recuperate''', you’ll crave / the life lived by the knave, behave!|title=‘Today’ (A Tetraquartet) and Other Poetry by Paul Millan|date=May 26 2026}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[restore]] (someone or something) to health, [[strength]], or [[currency]]; to [[revive]] or [[rehabilitate]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1901|author=Edward Harper Parker|title=China, Her History, Diplomacy, and Commerce: From the Earliest Times to the Present Day|publisher=London : Murray|page=191
|text=[...] of each province in 1842 and 1894 - that is, before the Taiping rebellion, and since China has '''recuperated''' her forces.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-03-09|author=Gary Day; Jack Lynch|title=The Encyclopedia of British Literature, 3 Volume Set: 1660 - 1789|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|isbn=9781444330205|page=494
|text=[...] one of many female poets who was trivialized and misrepresented for decades. When William Wordsworth '''recuperated''' her by praising her “Nocturnal Reverie,” he set what became a limiting factor in Finch's recovery: he treated her as a pre-Romantic ppoet of nature, and she became resituated in literary history as a much flatter or less complicated poet than she was in her lifetime.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[recover]]; to [[regain]].
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-08-01|author=Cristina Herrera; Paula Sanmartín|title=Reading/Speaking/Writing the Mother Text; Essays on Caribbean Women's Writing|publisher=Demeter Press|isbn=9781772580273
|text=In LS, July emerges as a survivor and a storyteller with a traumatic past who has '''recuperated''' her relationship with her lost son. Her questioning and humorously subversive discourse gives emotional and textual depth to {{nb...}}}}
#* {{see more citations|en}}
# {{lb|en|sociology}} To co-opt (a problematic or suspect idea) so that it becomes part of an accepted discourse; to [[reclaim]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991| title=Karl Mannheim and Hungarian Marxism |author=Joseph Gabel | page=87| passage=Mannheim's purpose when elaborating his typology of ideology was, as we have seen above, to '''recuperate''' the concept of ideology for scientific politics, after having discarded elements of Manichean egocentricity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999| title=Reconstituting the Body Politic|author=Jonathan M. Hess | page=24| passage=She sought ultimately to '''recuperate''' the classical concept of the public realm against what she described, in negative terms, as the "rise of the social" characteristic of the modern world.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Roger Beebe; Denise Fulbrook; Ben Saunders|title=Rock Over the Edge: Transformations in Popular Music Culture|passage={{...}} there is also the danger {{...}} that such a critique '''recuperates''' gender in terms that quite literally invisiblize the very issues of race and ethnicity {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020| title=Surroundings: A History of Environments and Environmentalisms|author=Etienne S. Benson | page=6| passage=The fact that even many of the harshest critics of environmental thought have sought to somehow '''recuperate''' the concept reflects how deeply it has become embedded in our discourse.}}

