===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|Ireland|Commonwealth|less common|_|in North America}} A [[line#English:_queue, especially for waiting|line]] of people, vehicles or other objects, usually one to be [[deal#Verb|dealt]] with in [[sequence]] (i.e., the one at the front end is dealt with first, the one behind is dealt with next, and so on), and which newcomers [[join]] at the opposite [[end]] (the [[back]]). {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1916|author=w:John Buchan|title=Greenmantle|chapter=[[s:Greenmantle/Chapter 5|Chapter 5]]
|passage=I was absent-minded at the moment and was last in the '''queue'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 November 15|author='Industry Insider'|title=Outbreak of common sense|journal=RAIL|issue=996|page=68|text=In a report published on October 31, Transport Focus said that a number of train companies were unable to convince it about their ability to sell a full range of tickets, handle cash payments, and avoid excessive '''queues''' at ticket machines.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|title=‘Geldof started flicking Vs at Farage’: the story of the Brexit campaign, told by those with a front-row seat|work=The Guardian|date=2026-06-23|url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/jun/23/brexit-campaign-oral-history-front-row-seat|issn=0261-3077|passage=George Osborne said: “In terms of getting an international trade deal with the United States, we would have to get to the back of the '''queue'''.” And Obama said: “Would it be helpful if I said that?”{{...|, and there was a mood that broadly it would}}. So he used the words in the press conference, and people said: “Well, that sounds like somebody’s told you to say that because you used the word ‘'''queue'''’ instead of ‘line’.” }}
# A [[waiting list]] or other means of [[organize|organizing]] people or objects into a first-come-first-served order.
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[data structure]] in which objects are added to one end, called the [[tail]], and removed from the other, called the [[head]] (in the case of a [[FIFO]] queue). The term can also refer to a [[LIFO]] ''queue'' or [[stack]] where these ends coincide. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* '''2005''', David Flanagan, ''Java in a Nutshell''<!--ISBN 0596007736-->, p. 234,
#*: '''Queue''' implementations are commonly based on insertion order as in first-in, first-out (FIFO) queues or last-in, first-out queues (LIFO queues are also known as stacks).
# {{lb|en|heraldry}} An animal's [[tail]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1863|author=Charles Boutell|title=A Manual of Heraldry|page=369
|passage=HESSE: Az., a lion, '''queue''' fourchée, rampt., barry of ten, arg. and gu., crowned, or, and holding in his dexter paw a sword, ppr., hilt and pommel, gold.}}
# {{senseid|en|hairstyle}} {{lb|en|now|_|historical}} A men's [[hairstyle]] with a [[braid]] or [[ponytail]] at the back of the head, such as that worn by men in Imperial [[China]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1869|author={{w|Alfred Russel Wallace}}|title={{w|The Malay Archipelago}}|publisher=Macmillan and Co.|year_published=|volume=II|location=London|page=213|passage=In the morning and evening, spruce Chinamen stroll about or chat at each other's doors, in blue trousers, white jacket, and a '''queue''' into which red silk is plaited till it reaches almost to their heels.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Micah Clarke|chapter=XIX|passage={{...}}, there were seated astraddle the whole hundred of the baronet's musqueteers, each engaged in plaiting into a '''queue''' the hair of the man who sat in front of him.}}
#* '''1912''', [[w:Herbert Allen Giles|Herbert Allen Giles]], ''China and the Manchus'', [[s:China_and_the_Manchus/Chapter_III|Chapter III — Shun Chih]]:
#*: A large number of loyal officials, rather than shave the front part of the head and wear the Manchu '''queue''', voluntarily shaved the whole head, {{...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1967|author=William Styron|title=The Confessions of Nat Turner|page=176|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2004
|passage=Caparisoned for a week in purple velvet knee-length pantaloons, a red silk jacket with buckles of shiny brass, and a white goat's-hair wig which culminated behind in a saucy '''queue''', I must have presented an exotic sight {{...}}}}

