===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|reren|t=to raise}}, from {{inh|en|ang|rǣran||to raise, set upright, promote, exalt, begin, create, give rise to, excite, rouse, arouse, stir up}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*raiʀijan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*raizijaną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*raisijaną|t=to cause to rise, raise}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁rey-||to lift oneself, rise}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|rere||to construct, build, rear}}, {{cog|is|reisa||to raise}}, {{cog|got|𐍂𐌰𐌹𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽||to cause to rise, lift up, establish}}, {{cog|de|reisen||to travel|lit=to rear up and depart}}; and a {{doublet|en|raise|nocap=1}}. More at {{l|en|rise}}.

Related to [[rise]] and [[raise]], which is used for several of its now archaic or obsolete senses and for some of its senses that are currently more common in other dialects of English.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|reer}}, {{l|en|rere}}, {{l|en|rare}} {{qual|all obsolete}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}
[[File:Dancing with horses (3).jpg|thumb|A '''rearing''' horse (3).]]

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bring up]] to maturity, as offspring; to [[educate]]; to [[instruct]]; to [[foster]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1694|author=w:Thomas Southerne|title=Isabella: Or The Fatal Marriage
|passage=He wants a father to protect his youth, and '''rear''' him up to virtue.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|said of people towards animals}} To [[breed]] and raise.
#: {{usex|en|The family has been '''rearing''' cattle for 200 years.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[rise up]] on the hind legs.
#: {{usex|en|The horse was shocked, and thus '''reared'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|usually with "up"}} To get angry.
#: {{ux|en|The skunk '''reared''' up on its hind legs when it faced a fox.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To rise high above, [[tower]] above.
#* {{RQ:Bramah Eyes of Max Carrados|passage=By my side '''reared''' a solid stone wall, and beneath was simply the room we stood in, for the space below the stairway was not enclosed.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|literary}} To [[raise]] physically or metaphorically; to lift up; to cause to rise, to elevate.
#: {{ux|en|Poverty '''reared''' its ugly head. {{gloss|appeared, started, began to have an effect}}}}
#: {{ux|en|The monster slowly '''reared''' its head.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=7|passage=In adoration at his feet I fell Submiss; he '''reared''' me.|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1835|author=w:Lord Lytton|title=Rienzi, the Last of the Roman Tribunes
|passage=Mine [shall be] the first hand to '''rear''' her banner.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To [[construct]] by building; to set up
#: {{co|en|to '''rear''' defenses or houses}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''rear''' one government on the ruins of another.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|prologue
|passage=One '''reared''' a font of stone.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To [[raise]] spiritually; to lift up; to [[elevate]] morally.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1700|author=w:Isaac Barrow|title=Of Industry...
|passage=It '''reareth''' our hearts from vain thoughts.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[lift]] and [[take up]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=III|canto=VIII|stanza=19|passage=And hauing her from Trompart lightly '''reard''', / Vpon his Courser set the louely lode,}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[rouse]]; to strip up.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1684|author=w:John Dryden|title=The Second Epode of Horace
|passage=And seeks the tusky boar to [[rear]].}}

=====Usage notes=====
* It is standard US English to {{m|en|raise}} children, and this usage has become common in all kinds of English since the 1700s. Until fairly recently, however, US teachers taught the traditional rule that one should {{m|en|raise}} crops and animals, but {{m|en|rear}} children, despite the fact that this contradicted general usage. It is therefore not surprising that some people still prefer to {{m|en|rear}} children and that this is considered correct but formal in US English. It is widespread in UK English and not considered formal.

* It is generally considered incorrect to rear crops or (adult) animals in US English, but this expression is common in UK English.

